From b2d974bdfa9f865eddeda47bdc2a29d5aa06b9ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: primez <mpincoski@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 05:16:42 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] [mpegts] Read the parameter sets of a stream only
 within their data

The HEVC parser read the NAL unit header and the last bits of a NAL
unit past the end of the data, when the data ended after a start code
or in a NAL unit. The bit reader now checks the end of the data before
it reads a byte for the emulation prevention check, and the parser
ignores a NAL unit that is shorter than its header.

The HEVC parser copied each parameter set with the 4 bytes before the
NAL unit, as for a 4 byte start code. After a 3 byte start code, the
copy started one byte early, before the buffer for the first unit, and
lost the last byte of the parameter set. The parser now takes the NAL
unit up to the next start code without trailing zero bytes, and the
extra data adds a 4 byte start code. The extra data also kept only the
parameter sets of one parse, so the VPS was lost when a TS packet ended
between the VPS and the PPS.

The H.264 parser kept the start of the last NAL unit only during one
parse. An SPS or PPS that continued in the next TS packet was copied
from a wrong position, and an SPS longer than 256 bytes overflowed a
stack buffer. The parser now keeps the start relative to the frame
start, and limits the SPS to the buffer.

A corrupt parameter set could have an id outside the parameter set
arrays, or H.264 SPS values outside the ranges of the standard. Then
the parser wrote outside the arrays, shifted by more than 32 bits, or
looped billions of times. The parsers now ignore such a parameter set.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
